2026-06-30 15:35:02,261 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-e2df557d-546f-424d-b038-501d5f4f2a7a', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nKL_L4_R2.raw\nKL_L4_R3.raw\nKL_L4_R4.raw\nKL_SAminus_R1.raw\nKL_SAminus_R2.raw\nKL_SAminus_R3.raw\nKL_SAplus_R1.raw\nKL_SAplus_R2.raw\nKL_SAplus_R3.raw\nKL_V3_R2.raw\nKL_V3_R3.raw\nKL_V3_R4.raw\nKL_YA_R1.raw\nKL_YA_R2.raw\nKL_YA_R3.raw\nKL_YE_R1.raw\nKL_YE_R2.raw\nKL_YE_R3.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nCharacterization of microbe-containing phagosomes using APEX2-based proximity labeling\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nPhagocytosis is the process by which myeloid phagocytes bind and internalize potentially dangerous microbes. During phagocytosis, innate immune receptors and the associated signaling adapters are localized to the maturing phagosome compartment. We used proximity labeling of phagosomal contents (PhagoPL) to identify proteins localizing to phagosomes containing model yeast and bacteria and identified programmed death-ligand 1 (PD-L1) as a protein that specifically enriches in phagosomes containing yeast. We found that PD-L1 directly binds to yeast upon processing in phagosomes and influences production of a subset of inflammatory cytokines and chemokines produced when macrophages encounter yeast.\n\n[METHODS]\nAPEX2-based PhagoPL BMDMs were seeded into 6-well-plates and primed overnight with 10 ng/ml mouse granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor (GM-CSF, PeproTech). The following day, cells were washed once with D-PBS. Opti-MEM media (Gibco) with 500 μM biotin-phenol (Sigma) and AEPX2-expressing microbes were added to the cells and the plates were centrifuged at 500g for 1 min before being incubated at 37°C to allow for 30 min of microbe phagocytosis by macrophages. The number of AEPX2-expressing microbes added was titrated in pilot experiments to ensure equal levels of total biotinylated protein determined by immunoblotting. At the end of incubation, epigallocatechin gallate (EGCG, Sigma) was added to 200 μM and 1 mM H2O2 was quickly added to initiate APEX2 labeling. After 1 min of labeling at room temperature (RT), cells were sequentially washed 3 times with quenching solution (10 mM sodium ascorbate, 5 mM Trolox and 10 mM sodium azide in D-PBS), 3 times with D-PBS, and lysed with 1% SDS lysis buffer (1% SDS, 50 mM Tris pH 7.5, 10 mM EDTA). Lysates were boiled at 95°C for 10 min and clarified by centrifugation at 12000g for 10 min at 4°C. 3 independent biological replicates were prepared for each of the microbes.  Preparation of APEX2-labeled proteins for mass spectrometry Streptavidin magnetic beads (Pierce) were washed with 0.05% SDS lysis buffer (0.05% SDS, 50 mM Tris pH 7.5, 10 mM EDTA). Lysates from APEX2-labled cell samples were diluted 1:20 into Tris-HCl buffer (50 mM Tris, pH7.5) and incubated with the washed beads for 90 min at RT with gentle mixing. Proteins bound to streptavidin beads were sequentially washed twice with 0.05% SDS lysis buffer, once with 1 M KCl, once with 0.1 M Na2CO3, once with 2 M urea in 10 mM Tris-HCl, once with 3 M urea in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate (ABC) buffer and once with 50 mM ABC buffer. Washed beads were resuspended in 50 mM ABC buffer and the bead-bound proteins were reduced with Bond-BreakerTM TCEP solution (5 mM, Thermo Fisher Scientific) at 55°C for 30 min. The samples were alkylated with 10 mM iodoacetamide (Sigma) for 20 min in the dark at 25°C with shaking. The reaction was then quenched upon adding DTT (20 mM final). The beads were subsequently washed twice with 2 M Urea in 50 mM ABC buffer before being digested overnight at 37°C with trypsin in 2 M urea (50 mM ABC buffer).  Label-free Proteomic Analysis Label-free proteomic comparison was conducted essentially as described (Yan et al., 2022). For each sample, 14 μL tryptic peptide solution was loaded onto a 2-cm trap column and then separated on a 50-cm EASY-Spray analytical column (Thermo Scientific) heated to 55°C, using a 3-h gradient at the flow rate of 250 nL/min. The resolved peptides were ionized by an EASY-Spray ion source (Thermo Scientific). Precursor and fragment mass spectra were acquired in a data-dependent manner in an Orbitrap Fusion Lumos mass spectrometer (Thermo Scientific). EASY-IC was used for internal mass calibration. Precursor ion scans were acquired in 240,000 resolution at m/z of 400 Th, with an ion packet setting of 4 × 105 for automatic gain control and a maximum injection time of 250 milliseconds (ms). Most intense peptide ions with charge state of 2-7 and mass-to-charge ratios of 375-1,650 were automatically selected for fragmentation by higher energy collisional dissociation, using 30% normalized collision energy. Tandem mass spectra were acquired in the ion trap, using rapid ion trap scan at 1 × 104 automatic gain control and a maximum injection time of 35 ms. To minimize redundant data acquisition, dynamic exclusion was enabled (repeat count of 1, exclusion during of 60s, and repeat during of 60s).\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nCandida albicans (yeast); Escherichia coli; Mus musculus (mouse)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
