ads were sequentially washed twice with 0.05% SDS lysis buffer, once with 1 M KCl, once with 0.1 M Na2CO3, once with 2 M urea in 10 mM Tris-HCl, once with 3 M urea in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate (ABC) buffer and once with 50 mM ABC buffer. Washed beads were resuspended in 50 mM ABC buffer and the bead-bound proteins were reduced with Bond-BreakerTM TCEP solution (5 mM, Thermo Fisher Scientific) at 55°C for 30 min. The samples were alkylated with 10 mM iodoacetamide (Sigma) for 20 min in the dark at 25°C with shaking. The reaction was then quenched upon adding DTT (20 mM final). The beads were subsequently washed twice with 2 M Urea in 50 mM ABC buffer before being digested overnight at 37°C with trypsin in 2 M urea (50 mM ABC buffer).  Label-free Proteomic Analysis Label-free proteomic comparison was conducted essentially as described (Yan et al., 2022). For each sample, 14 μL tryptic peptide solution was loaded onto a 2-cm trap column and then separated on a 50-cm EASY-Spray analytical column (Thermo Scientific) heated to 55°C, using a 3-h gradient at the flow rate of 250 nL/min. The resolved peptides were ionized by an EASY-Spray ion source (Thermo Scientific). Precursor and fragment mass spectra were acquired in a data-dependent manner in an Orbitrap Fusion Lumos mass spectrometer (Thermo Scientific). EASY-IC was used for internal mass calibration. Precursor ion scans were acquired in 240,000 resolution at m/z of 400 Th, with an ion packet setting of 4 × 105 for automatic gain control and a maximum injection time of 250 milliseconds (ms). Most intense peptide ions with charge state of 2-7 and mass-to-charge ratios of 375-1,650 were automatically selected for fragmentation by higher energy collisional dissociation, using 30% normalized collision energy. Tandem mass spectra were acquired in the ion trap, using rapid ion trap scan at 1 × 104 automatic gain control and a maximum injection time of 35 ms. To minimize redundant data acquisition, dynamic exclusion was ena